   "source": [
    "## Discussion and reflection\n",
    "At CPz, unrelated target words elicited a more negative deflection than related targets, emerging at approximately 280 ms after target onset, and largest around 350 ms. This closely resembled the N400 effect reported by @kappenman2021, who found an onset of ~285 ms, a peak latency of ~370 ms. The mean difference between conditions in the 300-500 ms window was approximately -7 μV (visually estimated from the graph), compared with -7.61 μV reported by @kappenman2021. The N400 was therefore successfully replicated. \n",
    "\n",
    "The grand average waveforms appeared slightly noisier than those in @kappenman2021, who applied a 20 Hz low-pass filter before plotting, whereas this investigation utilised a 30 Hz low-pass filter. Furthermore, participant 030 was excluded because no unrelated-word trials remained after artifact rejection, leaving N = 38 compared with N = 39 originally. This also suggests that the fixed thresholds were perhaps more conservative than the original pipeline. \n",
    "\n",
    "Working through the dataset showed me that the choice of time-locking event determines whether an effect appears at all. I had previously assumed that artifact rejection was a routine step, but the thresholds determined how many trials each participant kept and whether they stayed in the sample. I also learned that a replication does not require an identical pipeline, but it does require the same conditions, time-locking, and pattern of results. Next time I would check what each event label marks properly before choosing my conditions, because epoching the wrong condition would still produce a plot, but not the effect I was trying to replicate. Additionally, I would check how many trials each participant retains straight after epoching, because this would have shown me earlier that the fixed rejection thresholds were removing most of the data for some participants. \n",
